<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> there's no single best ski brand, but the major makers are Rossignol, Salomon, Atomic, K2, Völkl, Head, Blizzard, Nordica, Elan and Fischer. Each sells skis for beginners, groomers and powder, so pick the one whose lineup fits how and where you ski.</p></div>

<h2>How to pick a brand</h2>
<p>The ski matters more than the logo. Length, waist width, profile, and construction decide how a ski behaves, and nearly every brand below covers the full range from soft beginner skis to wide powder skis. Use brand to narrow your search, not as a ranking.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Start with your terrain.</strong> Some brands grew out of racing, others out of freeride or freestyle skiing, and their lineups still reflect that history.</li>
  <li><strong>Get your size first.</strong> The <%= link_to "ski size calculator", ski_size_chart_path %> gives you a length range from your height, weight, and ability.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a category.</strong> If you ski the whole mountain, our <%= link_to "all-mountain ski guide", guide_path("best-all-mountain-skis") %> explains waist widths and what to look for before you compare brands.</li>
  <li><strong>Learn the profile terms.</strong> Brands name their camber and rocker shapes differently. The <%= link_to "camber vs rocker guide", guide_path("camber-vs-rocker") %> explains what they do.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The major ski brands</h2>

<h3>Rossignol</h3>
<p>Abel Rossignol made his first wooden skis in his workshop in Voiron, France, in 1907. Laurent Boix-Vives bought the company in 1956, and it grew into one of the largest ski makers in the world. Rossignol makes skis for a wide range of skiers, from beginners to experts. <%= gear_link "Rossignol skis", "rossignol skis" %></p>

<h3>Salomon</h3>
<p>Salomon was founded in 1947 in Annecy, in the French Alps, where it started out making ski edges in a small family workshop. Adidas owned it from 1997 until 2005, when it was sold to Amer Sports. Today Salomon makes skis, boots, and bindings, along with a wide range of outdoor gear. <%= gear_link "Salomon skis", "salomon skis" %></p>

<h3>Atomic</h3>
<p>Alois Rohrmoser, a young wheelwright, founded Atomic in Austria's Salzburg region in 1955, and its main factory is in Altenmarkt im Pongau. Atomic has been part of Amer Sports since 1994. It makes skis, boots, and bindings, so it is easy to put together a full setup from one brand. <%= gear_link "Atomic skis", "atomic skis" %></p>

<h3>K2</h3>
<p>Brothers Bill and Don Kirschner founded K2 on Vashon Island, Washington, in 1962. K2 is known for pioneering fiberglass skis, which were lighter and livelier than the wood and metal skis of the time. The company moved its business office to Seattle in 2006. <%= gear_link "K2 skis", "k2 skis" %></p>

<h3>Völkl</h3>
<p>Völkl is based in Straubing, Bavaria, where the family business began producing skis in 1923. It is described as Germany's largest remaining ski manufacturer, and it still builds skis in Germany. <%= gear_link "Völkl skis", "volkl skis" %></p>

<h3>Head</h3>
<p>Aeronautical engineer Howard Head founded the Head Ski Company in Baltimore, Maryland, in 1950, and its Head Standard was one of the first successful metal-and-wood composite skis. Head builds skis in Kennelbach, Austria, and the company is also well known for tennis rackets. <%= gear_link "Head skis", "head skis" %></p>

<h3>Blizzard</h3>
<p>Anton "Toni" Arnsteiner built his first skis in a family carpentry workshop in Mittersill, Austria, in 1945. Blizzard became part of the Italian Tecnica Group in 2006–2007, and its skis are still designed and made in Mittersill. <%= gear_link "Blizzard skis", "blizzard skis" %></p>

<h3>Nordica</h3>
<p>Brothers Adriano and Oddone Vaccari founded Nordica in Montebelluna, Italy, in 1939. The brand made its name in ski boots, including early plastic and injection-molded designs, and it introduced its first skis in 2000. Nordica is part of the Tecnica Group, the same group as Blizzard. <%= gear_link "Nordica skis", "nordica skis" %></p>

<h3>Elan</h3>
<p>Elan was founded in 1945 in Begunje na Gorenjskem, Slovenia, and is still based there. It is known for the SCX, a ski with a much deeper sidecut than the skis of its day, which helped bring shaped, carving skis into the mainstream in the 1990s. <%= gear_link "Elan skis", "elan skis" %></p>

<h3>Fischer</h3>
<p>Josef Fischer Sr. founded Fischer in 1924 in Ried im Innkreis, Upper Austria, and the company is still headquartered there and family-owned. Fischer makes alpine skis and boots, and it is especially well known for cross-country (nordic) skis. <%= gear_link "Fischer skis", "fischer skis" %></p>

<h3>Line</h3>
<p>Jason Levinthal started Line in 1995 in New York while he was a college student, first making short skiboards. Line went on to help popularize full-length twin-tip skis for freestyle and park skiing. It's a common starting point for skiers who want a playful, freestyle-oriented ski. <%= gear_link "Line skis", "line skis" %></p>

<h3>Black Crows</h3>
<p>Pro skiers Camille Jaccoux and Bruno Compagnet founded Black Crows in Chamonix, France, in 2006. Its first model, the Corvus, launched in Chamonix that December. The brand focuses on freeride and backcountry skis, and the name refers to the alpine chough, a black bird common around Chamonix. <%= gear_link "Black Crows skis", "black crows skis" %></p>

<h3>DPS</h3>
<p>Stephan Drake and engineer Peter Turner founded DPS, short for Drake Powderworks, in Salt Lake City in 2005. The brand is known for carbon-fiber skis and for wide, rockered powder skis like its early Lotus 138. <%= gear_link "DPS skis", "dps skis" %></p>

<h2>Alternatives to Rossignol</h2>
<p>If you like Rossignol but want to compare, these are the closest matches. All of them sell skis across the same range, from beginner to all-mountain to powder, so pick by the kind of skiing you do.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Salomon</strong> is also French and also makes skis, boots, and bindings, so it's the easiest one-brand swap for a full setup.</li>
  <li><strong>Atomic</strong> (Austria) has a deep lineup from beginner skis to race skis, and also makes boots and bindings.</li>
  <li><strong>Völkl</strong> (Germany) and <strong>Head</strong> (Austria) suit skiers who want a damp, stable ski that holds an edge on hard snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Blizzard</strong> and <strong>Nordica</strong> (both Tecnica Group) are popular for all-mountain skis for intermediate and advanced skiers.</li>
  <li><strong>Dynastar</strong> is Rossignol's sister brand, owned by the same group, if you want something different from the same family.</li>
  <li><strong>Line</strong>, <strong>Black Crows</strong>, and <strong>DPS</strong> are the smaller-brand picks for freestyle, freeride, and powder skiing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not sure which type you need? Start with our <%= link_to "best all-mountain skis guide", guide_path("best-all-mountain-skis") %>, then compare two or three of these brands in your size.</p>

